# Module-wide options, all LANTERN_*.

menu "Lantern"

config LANTERN_KEYS
    bool "Paint the key LEDs from the palette, the strip and the keys from the lantern,keys node"
    default y
    depends on DT_HAS_LANTERN_KEYS_ENABLED
    select LED_STRIP
    select ZMK_LOW_PRIORITY_WORK_QUEUE
    help
      On a device with a lantern,keys node (a split half of the Cyboard
      Imprint in canon): the module is the one writer of the node's strip
      (src/keys.c). Each LED shows the palette slot the node assigns to the
      key under it (positions: the key under each LED, in chain order;
      slots: the slot of each key position), so the palette's colours fall
      on the keyboard in bands; a slot that is 0x000000 shows slot 0, and a
      device without a palette shows white. A frame is written when the LEDs
      light or go dark (LANTERN_ON_USB_POWER) and when the palette changes,
      on ZMK's low priority work queue, which this selects.

      CONFIG_ZMK_RGB_UNDERGLOW must be n on such a device (asserted): its
      tick would repaint the strip. The strip's chain-length must be the LED
      count, one positions entry each, and slots has one entry per key
      position of the keymap (both asserted). Default y where the node is
      present: the node is the switch, and a target without one (a dongle)
      builds none of the painting.

config LANTERN_BRIGHTNESS
    int "Brightness of the key LEDs: the brightest channel of every colour, in percent of full scale"
    range 1 100
    default 16
    depends on LANTERN_KEYS
    help
      Every palette colour is scaled so that its brightest channel is this
      percentage of full scale (255), which keeps its hue and saturation; a
      grey or black scales to white at that level. 16 is what ZMK's underglow
      drove on canon's halves before this module painted the strip
      (brightness 25 between Cyboard's 5 and 50).

config LANTERN_ON_USB_POWER
    bool "Light the key LEDs while USB powers this half, dark and unpowered on battery"
    default y
    depends on DT_HAS_LANTERN_RAIL_ENABLED
    depends on LANTERN_KEYS
    depends on GPIO
    help
      On a device with a lantern,rail node (a split half of the Cyboard
      Imprint in canon): while USB powers the device, the LED rail is on and
      the key LEDs show the palette's picture (LANTERN_KEYS); on battery the
      LEDs are black and the rail is off, so they draw nothing from the
      battery (src/on_usb_power.c). The rail is the node's gpios, the pin
      ZMK's zmk,ext-power-generic node would drive as control-gpios; that
      node stays disabled in the consumer's overlay (the source says why),
      and a gpio hog may hold the pin low from reset until the module takes
      it.

      Needs CONFIG_USB_DEVICE_STACK (ZMK's default on nRF52840, the split
      peripheral included) and CONFIG_SETTINGS; the source asserts each at
      build time, so a wrong conf fails the build with a message. Default y
      where the node is present: the node is the switch, and a target without
      one (a dongle) builds none of the module.

config LANTERN_BOOT_LIGHT_MS
    int "Light the key LEDs at boot for this many milliseconds (0 = no boot light)"
    range 0 60000
    default 1000
    depends on LANTERN_ON_USB_POWER
    help
      At boot the rail goes on and the key LEDs show the palette's picture
      for this long from its frame, on USB and on battery alike; then
      LANTERN_ON_USB_POWER's rule applies: on USB the LEDs stay lit, on
      battery they go black and the rail off. 0: no boot light, the rule
      applies at once.

config LANTERN_OFF_WHEN_IDLE
    bool "Dark once ZMK's activity state leaves ACTIVE, lit again on activity"
    default y
    depends on LANTERN_ON_USB_POWER
    help
      While USB powers the device, the LEDs go dark and the rail off, as on
      battery, once ZMK turns IDLE: CONFIG_ZMK_IDLE_TIMEOUT ms (ZMK's default
      30000) without a key, sensor or pointing input on this device, each
      split half for itself. The next input lights them again, settle-ms
      after the rail. The boot light is not cut short. The timeout is the
      consumer's line (canon's halves set 300000); a cable plugged into an
      idle device lights nothing until its next input (the source says why).

config LANTERN_COUNTDOWN
    bool "Count down to the idle dark in the palette's colours, a stage a colour"
    default y
    depends on LANTERN_OFF_WHEN_IDLE
    depends on LANTERN_PALETTE
    help
      While the key LEDs are lit, each half counts the time since its last
      key in stages of LANTERN_COUNTDOWN_STAGE_MS and changes the picture at
      each: the bands, then the whole half in each colour of the palette in
      turn (a cleared slot is slot 0's colour; colours that paint alike at
      LANTERN_BRIGHTNESS count once), then the bands again, until ZMK turns
      idle and LANTERN_OFF_WHEN_IDLE blanks the LEDs. A key brings the bands
      back and restarts the clock; a cable plugged in lights the stage the
      keys say (src/keys.c). A sensor or pointing input counts for ZMK's idle
      and not for this clock. One colour: the bands until the dark.

config LANTERN_COUNTDOWN_STAGE_MS
    int "Milliseconds between two pictures of the countdown"
    range 100 3600000
    default 10000
    depends on LANTERN_COUNTDOWN
    help
      How long each picture of the countdown shows before the next: the
      bands, then each colour of the palette, then the bands again, until
      ZMK's idle blanks the LEDs. Ten seconds is what canon's user chose on
      hardware (2026-10-20, t-x82q); a minute a colour, with the colours
      running out at the idle timeout, read as still.

config LANTERN_PALETTE
    bool "Keep a palette of six colours, set from the split central's host; the key LEDs show them"
    default y
    depends on DT_HAS_LANTERN_BEHAVIOR_PALETTE_ENABLED
    depends on SETTINGS
    help
      Six colours (0xRRGGBB, slots 0..5) that the host hands the split
      central (LANTERN_PALETTE_PORT) and that the central and every half keep
      in their settings ("lantern/palette/colours"); a half's key LEDs show
      them in the bands its lantern,keys node draws (LANTERN_KEYS), repainted
      when a palette arrives while they are lit and shown at every boot. A
      set of fewer than six colours clears the rest to 0x000000, which the
      LEDs show as slot 0. The central's code invokes the
      lantern,behavior-palette node, which ZMK's split transport carries to
      every half by its name: the node is named palette on every device
      (src/palette.c, the binding), one pair of slots an invocation, three
      a palette. On a split central the palette is resent to a
      half at each of its battery reports, the first of which follows every
      (re)connection, so a half that was away gets it when it is back: that
      needs CONFIG_ZMK_SPLIT_BLE_CENTRAL_BATTERY_LEVEL_FETCHING, asserted at
      build time; and its run queue must hold a whole palette for every
      half at once, CONFIG_ZMK_SPLIT_BLE_CENTRAL_SPLIT_RUN_QUEUE_SIZE at
      least 3 x the peripheral count (6 for two halves; ZMK's default is 5),
      asserted too. Default y where the node is present: the node is the
      switch, as the lantern,rail node is for LANTERN_ON_USB_POWER.

config LANTERN_PALETTE_PORT
    bool "Take palette commands on the split central's USB CDC ACM port"
    default y
    depends on LANTERN_PALETTE
    depends on ZMK_SPLIT_ROLE_CENTRAL
    depends on USB_CDC_ACM
    select UART_INTERRUPT_DRIVEN
    help
      A text line "palette RRGGBB [RRGGBB ...]" (one to six colours) on any
      CDC ACM port of the split central sets slots 0.. on every half and on
      the central and clears the rest, answered with "palette ok" and the six
      slots; "palette" or
      "palette?" asks for them (src/palette_port.c is the whole grammar).
      canon's scripts/dongle.py palette sends it to the Imprint Dongle. The
      port's rate is untouched: 1200 baud is zmk-beacon's bootloader entry.
      The reply is written without waiting for a reader; a line that does
      not begin with "palette" gets none. It takes the UART interrupt
      callback of every CDC ACM port, of which a device keeps one: a
      consumer that gives a port to a shell or another reader sets this n.

endmenu
